# Student Notes: Upload a Website to GitHub Using Git

**Topic:** Git & GitHub Practical  
**Objective:** Upload HTML, CSS, and JavaScript files to a GitHub repository.

## 1. Prerequisites

Install or configure the following:

- **Visual Studio Code** – To create and edit website files.
- **Git** – To track code changes.
- **GitHub account** – To store and share your code online.
- **GitHub CLI (optional)** – To authenticate with GitHub using the terminal.

Check whether Git is installed:

```powershell
git --version
```

Example output:

```text
git version 2.54.0.windows.1
```

## 2. Create Website Files

Create a folder named `DIV` and add these three files:

```text
DIV/
├── index.html
├── style.css
└── script.js
```

- `index.html` – Website structure.
- `style.css` – Website design and styling.
- `script.js` – Website functionality.

## 3. Open the Project Folder in PowerShell

Navigate to your project folder:

```powershell
cd "C:\Users\TECHVITY\OneDrive\Desktop\DIV"
```

Check the files:

```powershell
dir
```

## 4. Initialize Git

Initialize a Git repository inside the project folder:

```powershell
git init
```

Check the repository status:

```powershell
git status
```

**Note:** If you see `fatal: not a git repository`, navigate to the correct project folder and run `git init` if the folder has not already been initialized.

## 5. Add Files to Git

Add all three files:

```powershell
git add index.html script.js style.css
```

Alternatively, add all changes in the current folder:

```powershell
git add .
```

Verify the staged files:

```powershell
git status
```

## 6. Commit Your Changes

Create a commit with a descriptive message:

```powershell
git commit -m "website upload"
```

A commit saves a snapshot of your staged changes in your local Git repository.

## 7. Create a GitHub Repository

1. Open https://github.com/
2. Sign in to your GitHub account.
3. Click **New repository**.
4. Enter the repository name: `DIV`.
5. Select the appropriate visibility, such as Public or Private.
6. Create the repository.

Copy the repository URL. For this example:

```text
https://github.com/saitejabalareddypalli/DIV.git
```

Replace the username and repository name with your own details.

## 8. Connect Your Local Project to GitHub

Add the GitHub repository as the remote named `origin`:

```powershell
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/DIV.git
```

Check the remote:

```powershell
git remote -v
```

Rename the current branch to `main`:

```powershell
git branch -M main
```

## 9. Authenticate with GitHub

If GitHub rejects your push because of authentication or permissions, verify that you are using the correct GitHub account.

If GitHub CLI is installed, run:

```powershell
gh auth login
```

Follow the prompts:

1. Select GitHub.com.
2. Select HTTPS.
3. Choose browser authentication.
4. Complete the login in your browser.

Check the authenticated account:

```powershell
gh auth status
```

**Important:** You must have permission to push to the target GitHub repository. Logging into an account that does not have access will not fix a repository permission error.

## 10. Push the Website to GitHub

Upload your local commits:

```powershell
git push -u origin main
```

If the push succeeds, GitHub will show your files in the repository.

The `-u` option sets the upstream branch so that future pushes can normally use:

```powershell
git push
```

## 11. Update the Website After Making Changes

Whenever you edit your HTML, CSS, or JavaScript files, run:

```powershell
git status
git add .
git commit -m "Update website"
git push
```

These commands:

1. Check the changes.
2. Stage the updated files.
3. Create a new commit.
4. Upload the commit to GitHub.

## 12. Common Errors and Solutions

| Error | Reason | Solution |
|---|---|---|
| `git: 'v' is not a git command` | Incorrect command | Use `git --version` |
| `not a git repository` | Git is not initialized in the current folder, or you are in the wrong folder | Navigate to the correct folder and run `git init` if needed |
| `script.js is not recognized` | The filename was entered as a separate PowerShell command | Use `git add index.html script.js style.css` on one line |
| `src refspec main does not match any` | The branch name is incorrect or no commit exists on that branch | Commit your files and check the branch using `git branch` |
| `Permission denied (403)` | The authenticated account lacks permission to push | Sign in with the correct account or request repository access |
| `remote origin already exists` | A remote named `origin` is already configured | Check it with `git remote -v`; use `git remote set-url origin YOUR_REPOSITORY_URL` if necessary |
| `rejected — fetch first` | The remote contains commits missing locally | Review and integrate the remote changes before pushing; do not overwrite them blindly |

## 13. Important Git Commands

| Command | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `git --version` | Check Git version |
| `git init` | Initialize a repository |
| `git status` | Check file and repository status |
| `git add .` | Stage changes |
| `git commit -m "message"` | Save a local commit |
| `git remote -v` | View remote repositories |
| `git branch` | View local branches |
| `git branch -M main` | Rename the current branch to `main` |
| `git push -u origin main` | Push commits and set upstream |
| `git push` | Push new commits to the configured upstream |
| `git log --oneline` | View recent commits |

## 14. Practical Assignment for Students

Complete the following tasks:

- [ ] Create a folder named `MyWebsite`.
- [ ] Create `index.html`, `style.css`, and `script.js`.
- [ ] Initialize Git in the project folder.
- [ ] Add and commit the files.
- [ ] Create a GitHub repository.
- [ ] Connect the local project to GitHub.
- [ ] Push the code to the `main` branch.
- [ ] Make one small change and push the update.
- [ ] Share your GitHub repository URL with your trainer.

**Expected outcome:** Students should be able to upload a basic website to GitHub and update it whenever the code changes.

**Remember:** Git stores and tracks your code; GitHub hosts the repository online. Uploading code to GitHub does not automatically make a website live. To publish the website, configure a hosting service such as GitHub Pages separately.
